Workout and Meal Planning Apps: Build the Backend with PostgreSQL and REST APIs

A code-focused implementation guide covering relational models, workout APIs, meal calculations, data integrity, authorization, and testing.

A workout and meal planning app looks straightforward in the UI: choose an exercise, log a set, plan breakfast, and review progress. The backend is more demanding. It must distinguish templates from completed activities, preserve historical measurements, enforce record ownership, and calculate nutrition using consistent units.

This guide uses PostgreSQL, Python/FastAPI-style endpoints, and SQLAlchemy examples to show a practical approach. The snippets illustrate patterns; they are not a complete production application. Authentication configuration, migrations, exception handling, and deployment infrastructure must be added before release.
1. Model the data before creating endpoints
Keep reusable catalog entries separate from user-owned records:
users
 ├── workout_plans ─── workout_plan_exercises ─── exercises
 ├── workout_sessions ─── workout_sets ─────────── exercises
 ├── meal_plans ─────── scheduled_meals ───────── recipes
 └── progress_records                           └─ recipe_ingredients ── foods
A workout plan says what is intended; a workout session says what happened. A recipe describes possible food portions; a meal log should preserve what was recorded at the time, even if the source food database changes later.
PostgreSQL schema
The following is a deliberately compact foundation. It requires PostgreSQL with gen_random_uuid() available.
CREATE TABLE users (
    id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),
    email TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE,
    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT now()
);

CREATE TABLE exercises (
    id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),
    name TEXT NOT NULL,
    category TEXT NOT NULL
);

CREATE TABLE workout_plans (
    id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),
    user_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES users(id),
    title TEXT NOT NULL,
    version INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 1 CHECK (version > 0),
    updated_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT now()
);

CREATE TABLE workout_plan_exercises (
    id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),
    plan_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES workout_plans(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
    exercise_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES exercises(id),
    position INTEGER NOT NULL CHECK (position >= 0),
    target_sets INTEGER NOT NULL CHECK (target_sets > 0),
    target_reps INTEGER CHECK (target_reps > 0),
    UNIQUE (plan_id, position)
);

CREATE TABLE workout_sessions (
    id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),
    user_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES users(id),
    plan_id UUID REFERENCES workout_plans(id),
    plan_snapshot JSONB NOT NULL,
    started_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),
    completed_at TIMESTAMPTZ,
    CHECK (completed_at IS NULL OR completed_at >= started_at)
);

CREATE TABLE workout_sets (
    id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),
    session_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES workout_sessions(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
    exercise_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES exercises(id),
    set_number INTEGER NOT NULL CHECK (set_number > 0),
    reps INTEGER CHECK (reps >= 0),
    weight_kg NUMERIC(8, 2) CHECK (weight_kg >= 0),
    UNIQUE (session_id, exercise_id, set_number)
);

CREATE INDEX idx_workout_plans_user ON workout_plans(user_id);
CREATE INDEX idx_workout_sessions_user_time
    ON workout_sessions(user_id, started_at DESC);
Why use plan_snapshot? If a user later changes their template from three sets to four, a completed session must not silently change. Save the actual plan configuration at session creation and the completed results separately. For repeated exercises or supersets, model session exercise instances rather than identifying them only by exercise_id.
2. Define predictable workout REST endpoints
Method	Endpoint	Purpose
GET	/api/v1/exercises	Paginated exercise catalog
POST	/api/v1/workout-plans	Create a plan and ordered exercises
GET	/api/v1/workout-plans/{id}	Retrieve a plan the user owns
PUT	/api/v1/workout-plans/{id}	Replace plan with version checking
POST	/api/v1/workout-sessions	Start a session with a plan snapshot
POST	/api/v1/workout-sessions/{id}/sets	Record completed sets
GET	/api/v1/progress	Read historical records


Example request and response
POST /api/v1/workout-plans
Authorization: Bearer <access-token>
Content-Type: application/json
{
  "title": "Upper Body Strength",
  "exercises": [
    {
      "exercise_id": "4c621dad-3877-46e5-8fa3-55e21bb03b1d",
      "position": 0,
      "target_sets": 3,
      "target_reps": 10
    }
  ]
}
A successful creation returns 201 Created and a Location header pointing to the new plan. Validate exercise IDs, duplicate positions, and valid target values. Insert the parent plan and child rows in one database transaction.
3. Implement typed request validation with FastAPI
FastAPI and Pydantic make constraints explicit at the API boundary:
from uuid import UUID
from pydantic import BaseModel, Field

class PlannedExercise(BaseModel):
    exercise_id: UUID
    position: int = Field(ge=0)
    target_sets: int = Field(gt=0, le=30)
    target_reps: int | None = Field(default=None, gt=0, le=1000)

class CreateWorkoutPlan(BaseModel):
    title: str = Field(min_length=1, max_length=120)
    exercises: list[PlannedExercise] = Field(min_length=1)
This is only the first layer of validation. The database should enforce foreign keys and uniqueness, while application services enforce ownership and business rules.
Ownership checks in the database query
For protected objects, never look up an item by ID and assume a valid login grants access.
from uuid import UUID
from sqlalchemy import text
from sqlalchemy.orm import Session

def get_owned_plan(db: Session, plan_id: UUID, user_id: UUID):
    return db.execute(
        text("""
            SELECT id, title, version
            FROM workout_plans
            WHERE id = :plan_id AND user_id = :user_id
        """),
        {"plan_id": plan_id, "user_id": user_id},
    ).mappings().one_or_none()
user_id must be taken from a verified authenticated identity provided by your auth middleware/dependency; never trust a client-supplied user ID. A trainer-client relationship needs its own explicit authorization model.
Create the plan atomically
from sqlalchemy import text
from sqlalchemy.orm import Session


def create_workout_plan(db: Session, user_id, data: CreateWorkoutPlan):
    positions = [item.position for item in data.exercises]
    if len(positions) != len(set(positions)):
        raise ValueError("Exercise positions must be unique")

    with db.begin():
        plan_id = db.execute(
            text("""
                INSERT INTO workout_plans (user_id, title)
                VALUES (:user_id, :title)
                RETURNING id
            """),
            {"user_id": user_id, "title": data.title},
        ).scalar_one()

        for item in data.exercises:
            db.execute(
                text("""
                    INSERT INTO workout_plan_exercises
                    (plan_id, exercise_id, position, target_sets, target_reps)
                    VALUES (:plan_id, :exercise_id, :position,
                            :target_sets, :target_reps)
                """),
                {"plan_id": plan_id, **item.model_dump()},
            )
    return plan_id
Use a request-scoped SQLAlchemy session that is not already inside a transaction for this example. A foreign-key or uniqueness failure rolls back the plan and all inserted exercises. Map known database errors to useful HTTP responses instead of exposing SQL exception messages.
4. Record actual workout results—not just planned targets
For a completed set, the request might be:
POST /api/v1/workout-sessions/8d9f8e48-376b-4810-b8ab-0d1e7f5baa98/sets
{
  "exercise_id": "4c621dad-3877-46e5-8fa3-55e21bb03b1d",
  "set_number": 1,
  "reps": 10,
  "weight_kg": 35.5
}
On the server:
1. Verify the authenticated user owns the session.
2. Verify the session is not completed or locked.
3. Verify the exercise is permitted in this session's snapshot, or apply the documented ad-hoc-exercise rule.
4. Insert the set and return 201 Created.
5. Use an idempotency strategy for retries, and a unique database key to prevent duplicate set records.
Do not overwrite past sessions when templates change. If editing historical records is supported, record the correction separately or maintain an audit trail.
5. Store food nutrition with explicit units
Food portions become unreliable if one entry means “one serving,” another means “100 g,” and a third refers to cooked weight without saying so.

A minimal nutrition table can use a consistent per-100-gram basis:
CREATE TABLE foods (
    id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),
    name TEXT NOT NULL,
    source_name TEXT NOT NULL,
    source_item_id TEXT,
    energy_kcal_per_100g NUMERIC(10, 3),
    protein_g_per_100g NUMERIC(10, 3),
    carbs_g_per_100g NUMERIC(10, 3),
    fat_g_per_100g NUMERIC(10, 3),
    CHECK (energy_kcal_per_100g >= 0),
    CHECK (protein_g_per_100g >= 0),
    CHECK (carbs_g_per_100g >= 0),
    CHECK (fat_g_per_100g >= 0)
);

CREATE TABLE recipes (
    id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),
    user_id UUID REFERENCES users(id),
    title TEXT NOT NULL
);

CREATE TABLE recipe_ingredients (
    recipe_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES recipes(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
    food_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES foods(id),
    ingredient_position INTEGER NOT NULL CHECK (ingredient_position >= 0),
    grams NUMERIC(12, 3) NOT NULL CHECK (grams > 0),
    PRIMARY KEY (recipe_id, ingredient_position)
);
Store where nutrition values came from. Raw and cooked ingredient forms need distinct data or valid conversion rules. Missing values must not silently become zero.
Calculate nutrition using decimal arithmetic
from decimal import Decimal, ROUND_HALF_UP


def nutrient_for_portion(per_100g: Decimal, grams: Decimal) -> Decimal:
    if grams <= 0:
        raise ValueError("Portion must be positive")
    if per_100g < 0:
        raise ValueError("Nutrient value cannot be negative")
    value = (per_100g * grams) / Decimal("100")
    return value.quantize(Decimal("0.01"), rounding=ROUND_HALF_UP)

protein = nutrient_for_portion(Decimal("12"), Decimal("150"))
assert protein == Decimal("18.00")
Round only at defined calculation/display boundaries. For recipes, sum correctly converted ingredients and divide by the actual number of portions. If a recipe or reference food changes later, previously logged meals should retain the nutrition snapshot and source information originally used.

